00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:46.100
but enough sort of personal fortitude

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:50.960
and patience and all of it
to just be able to sit there

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.120
and put all of those
thoughts out 16 times?

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:55.440
And quite successfully, by the way.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.620
It's not like nobody's
reading these things.

00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:59.430
- Yeah, well, thank
you, I appreciate that.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:00.970
Well, first of all, I'm 65,

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:03.887
so I've got a couple of
decades on you or almost,

00:02:03.887 --> 00:02:05.190
(laughing)

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:08.060
just cumulative time, but I
also started writing in my 20s.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:09.570
When I was in college, actually,

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:11.010
I always wanted to be a writer.

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:13.210
And then by the time
I got to my early 30s,

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:16.167
I had written quite a bit
that wasn't published,

00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:18.530
just practicing stuff and
trying to get things published,

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:22.580
such that by my first
book, let's see, in 1997,

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:27.437
so I was 30, let's see, 33,
no, let's see, no, I was 43,

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.103
I had quite a bit of material.

00:02:32.481 --> 00:02:33.983
- 43 at my first book, I'm with you.

00:02:33.983 --> 00:02:36.350
- See, see, that's right.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:39.900
So each book kinda ends
with a question of sorts,

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.850
or in my mind anyway,
that's the next book.

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:45.950
And it's also a strategy
when you're cutting,

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:48.140
'cause I usually write too much material,

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:50.160
and then I think, "Oh, I really
don't wanna give this up,"

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.087
and I can talk myself
into doing that by saying,

00:02:52.087 --> 00:02:53.500
"I'll put it in the next book,"

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.110
and then it shortens that book.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.460
So you don't feel like
you've wasted your time.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:01.320
By temperament, I feel
like if I don't write

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.760
pretty much every day, it's
like if I don't go work out,

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:05.730
if I don't ride my bike, I feel anxious,

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:09.550
like I really need to write,
I just have a need for it.

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:12.540
I know other people, like
Isaac Asimov is that way,

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:13.980
apparently Stephen King is like that.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.090
They just gotta crank out the
material 'cause it feels good.

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:18.790
It's just sort of what I do,

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:21.063
like an artist paints, I write, whatever.

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:25.190
So I find a lot it's not a chore.

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:29.413
I don't have writer's block,
per se, I have time block.

00:03:30.388 --> 00:03:32.388
I just need more time 'cause I enjoy it.

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:35.100
- One of the things that
I really like that you do,

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:36.540
and it's not just in this book,

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:38.460
it's in really all of your books,

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.200
or at least the last few
since I've known you,

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:43.650
is you give credit to a lot
of the other current thinkers

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.020
that are talking about these things,

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:47.670
and I think that's actually
a kinda unique trait,

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:49.660
because a lotta people wanna sit there

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:52.240
and make it seem like they
came up with all of this.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:55.020
But even I was looking at the blurbs

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:55.853
in the back of the book,

00:03:55.853 --> 00:03:59.250
and actually it's a Rubin
Report reunion over here

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:01.330
'cause you've got Stephen
Pinker, Jordan Peterson,

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:04.320
Nadine Strossen, Nick
Christakis, Amy Chua,

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.710
and Jonathan Haidt, and these
are people that you reference.

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:08.861
- And they've all been on your show.

00:04:08.861 --> 00:04:11.470
- No, they've all been on
the show, which is nice,

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:13.740
but that is one of the things
that I like about this book

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:17.040
because you reference a lotta
these people and their ideas

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.860
as sort of the starting
point or maybe midpoint

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.510
for how you think about things.

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:24.940
Were you always that way,
that you would hear somebody

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:28.610
and kinda mirror what
you think against them?

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:32.370
- Yeah, to a certain
extent, but as a writer

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.270
and as a consumer of many other books,

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:36.060
I can see some authors are more generous

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:39.340
about crediting where
their ideas came from

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:40.790
and sort of tracking for the reader

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:43.860
how they came up with their
thesis of the book or whatever.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:44.840
Others not so much.

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:45.900
I'm not gonna name any names,

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.340
but some people do write in a way

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.760
like they just came out
of this whole cloth,

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:52.313
it just popped into my
brain, like the muses.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.140
No ideas happen like that.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:57.890
We all build on each
other, everybody does.

00:04:57.890 --> 00:04:59.660
In scholarly papers, you have to cite

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:02.063
just tons and tons and tons of references,

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.480
and that's why sometimes
the reference sections

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:07.330
are as long as the papers themselves.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:10.800
So I always felt like,
well, I wanna be generous

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.920
and just honest and say
here's where I came up

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:15.800
with these ideas 'cause
of these three authors

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:18.260
or these 16 sources, or whatever.

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:20.490
Anyway, so I just got in
the habit of doing that

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:24.160
just from training as an
experimental psychologist initially

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:26.560
and then a historian of science,
you just have to do that.

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:29.800
But just personally, I
feel when I see that,

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.210
someone like Richard Dawkins
is very generous about that,

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:35.420
as is Steve Pinker, and of deciding

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:37.810
where all these ideas come
from, quoting other people.

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:39.150
And it's like, I like that,

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:40.813
I wanna make sure I do that, too.

00:05:42.729 --> 00:05:44.430
- So one other thing
before we dive specifically

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:46.170
into the topics in the book,

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:47.890
because you have now been
on "The Rubin Report"

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:51.360
at least six times, which
places you at number one,

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.560
which I'm sure despite
your rather lofty resume,

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:56.990
I'm sure that's clearly
at the top of the list.

00:05:56.990 --> 00:05:58.820
But for somebody that's
watching this right now

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:00.770
that just doesn't know anything about you,

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:04.070
can you just give me a
two or three minute recap

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:07.480
on how you kinda became
the skeptic's skeptic?

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:10.960
- Yeah, well, my day job is
publishing "Skeptic" magazine

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.780
and directing the Skeptic
Society, which is a 501(c)(3)

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:16.410
nonprofit science education organization.

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:18.190
I got interested in the topis,

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:20.870
which is pretty much any of
the super interesting topics

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:23.590
related to science and
religion and spirituality

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:26.623
and cults and free will
and determinism and God.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:30.180
Pretty much all the great
topics I'm interested in.

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:32.010
It really goes back to being in college,

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:33.660
and I was a born-again Christian.

00:06:35.039 --> 00:06:39.060
In 1971, when I was in 11th
grade, I had two close friends

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:40.710
who were interested in converting me,

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:42.733
I didn't even know what
that meant, really,

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:45.013
George and Frank.

00:06:45.013 --> 00:06:47.980
So George had a really cute
sister and invited me to church.

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:50.187
I thought, well, okay, here's my chance.

00:06:50.187 --> 00:06:51.020
(laughing)

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:52.520
But then when I got to the church,

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:56.260
it was this Presbyterian
church in Glendale, California,

00:06:56.260 --> 00:07:00.430
and the minister was unbelievably dynamic

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:03.137
and just really motivating,
and at the end he said,

00:07:03.137 --> 00:07:04.967
"Come on up and you can accept Jesus,

00:07:04.967 --> 00:07:06.450
"the whole thing will happen right here,"

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:08.470
and I'm like, all right, I'm going.

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:10.770
So I went, but I didn't even
know what I doing, really.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:12.840
But I did it and then
took it pretty seriously.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:15.270
And then I went to Pepperdine University,

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:18.520
which is a Church of Christ
school and very conservative.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:21.300
Gerald Ford came to speak
and Edward Teller came

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:23.400
and gave his mutual
assured destruction speech,

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:26.920
and everyone was, Ayn Rand,
"Atlas Shrugged" and all that,

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:29.470
pretty much what you'd expect.

00:07:29.470 --> 00:07:31.510
But then when I went to graduate school,

00:07:31.510 --> 00:07:34.910
I kinda drifted away from being religious,

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:36.480
mainly for two reasons.

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:41.120
One, studying social psychology,
anthropology, sociology,

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:43.990
I could see that other people's
religious belief systems

00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:47.580
are very much context-dependent,
geographically-dependent.

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:49.240
So had I been born in India,

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:51.470
I very likely would not be Christian.

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:53.760
Had I been born 10,000 years ago,

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:55.630
there would be no Christians, right?

00:07:55.630 --> 00:08:00.280
So it seemed to me that
that doesn't make it wrong,

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:04.050
but it makes it more likely
to be mythically true,

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:06.140
as our friend Jordan Peterson would say,

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:08.830
not empirically true.

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:10.070
- [Dave] We're gonna get into that.

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:13.570
- Yeah, yeah, but then
also the problem of evil,

00:08:13.570 --> 00:08:17.140
the theodicy, why bad things
happen to good people.

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:19.980
And I'm not talking about
like a human volition

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:21.810
of homicide or war or something like that,

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:24.690
but just like innocent
children dying of leukemia

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:26.310
or thousands of children dying

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:28.780
from starvation every year, and so on.

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:30.760
It's like, why would a good
God allow that to happen?

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:32.700
I know there's tons of books and papers

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:35.020
by theists and theologians about this,

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:36.580
but to me they've never
squared that circle,

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:38.170
they've never solved that problem,

00:08:38.170 --> 00:08:41.167
other than like Rabbi
Kushner's famous book,

00:08:41.167 --> 00:08:43.520
"When Bad Things Happen to Good People."

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:46.950
His conclusion was God simply
can't do anything about it.

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:48.160
He's not omniscient.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:50.010
I mean, he's not omnipotent.

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:52.640
He may know everything, but
he can't stop evil, okay.

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:53.820
So that's one solution.

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:56.363
Another one is, well,
there just is no God.

00:08:58.208 --> 00:08:59.847
And that's the simpler solution to me,

00:08:59.847 --> 00:09:01.010
and that explains a lot,

00:09:01.010 --> 00:09:02.720
because if you understand
the laws of physics

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:05.390
and say the Second Law of
Thermodynamics, entropy,

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:07.070
just shit happens.

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:08.830
It's mostly just random (chuckles)

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:12.380
and there's very little
directionality to why things happen

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:14.930
other than just statistical probabilities,

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:17.083
and that's it, there's nothing to explain.

00:09:19.335 --> 00:09:21.530
So like the coronavirus, what's the cause?

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:22.890
500 years ago it would have been,

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:24.730
well, women have been
cavorting with demons

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:26.990
in the middle of the night,
or the Jews poisoned the well,

00:09:26.990 --> 00:09:30.740
or some of these crazy
supernatural paranormal,

00:09:30.740 --> 00:09:34.410
and a racist, bigoted
targeting of peoples.

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:38.070
Well, we don't do that anymore,
except for the 5G craziness,

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:39.930
because we live in an age of science

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:41.840
and hopefully we've moved past that.

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:46.840
So to me, once I left the
church and, really, religion,

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:50.350
I was still interested in
answering the big questions,

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:52.060
like, well, then where
did the universe came from

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:52.893
if there's no God?

00:09:52.893 --> 00:09:54.810
What's the origins of morality?

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:56.680
What about consciousness?

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:58.660
Why is the universe tuned the way it is?

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:01.610
Where did all this
diversity of life come from?

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:04.470
All the great questions
from a secular perspective.

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:06.820
So in trying to answer
it myself, I thought,

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:09.420
well, I wanna write, so this
is what I'm gonna write about

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:10.860
from a skeptical perspective.

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:13.590
So my little niche, my lane as they say,

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:16.970
is kind of on the margins
of science, pseudoscience,

00:10:16.970 --> 00:10:19.470
and the paranormal, the supernatural,

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:22.240
the controversial stuff that
most mainstream scientists

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:23.320
don't deal with.

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:26.370
That's my lane, and
from there I can tackle

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:30.040
the great questions, just from
my particular perspective.

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:31.587
So like I have a chapter in the book,

00:10:31.587 --> 00:10:33.420
"Mr. Hume, Tear Down this Wall,"

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:36.060
and the one before it
defending scientific humanism.

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:38.980
The point of that is
I'm trying to get away

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:42.950
from this kind of NOMA,
as Steve Gould called it,

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:46.010
non-overlapping magisteria,
where Gould argued,

00:10:46.010 --> 00:10:47.983
Most scientists tend to go this way,

00:10:49.030 --> 00:10:51.160
that science and religion
are two different things,

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:53.830
and that you scientists
get the empirical world

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:55.630
and the religious people,
they get the world

00:10:55.630 --> 00:10:57.490
of morality and meaning and purpose.

00:10:57.490 --> 00:10:59.030
And I'm like, wait a minute.

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:01.100
Why can't we say something about that?

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:02.810
I mean, I don't believe in God,

00:11:02.810 --> 00:11:05.470
but I still believe that there
are objective moral values.

00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:07.760
How can that be, 'cause
there's no outside source?

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:08.780
Well, here's how it could be.

00:11:08.780 --> 00:11:10.350
So I try to defend that.

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:12.220
Now, there is a technical problem

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:13.730
called the naturalistic fallacy

