00:22:27.660 --> 00:22:29.933
We're drawing a line in
the sand, this is it.

00:22:31.150 --> 00:22:34.510
But they've abandoned that in a way.

00:22:34.510 --> 00:22:37.290
I think an explanation might
come from Karl Popper's,

00:22:37.290 --> 00:22:38.700
the great philosopher of science,

00:22:38.700 --> 00:22:40.980
Karl Popper's book, "The Open Society."

00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:42.120
And so he made the argument

00:22:42.120 --> 00:22:44.470
that there's something of a paradox

00:22:44.470 --> 00:22:48.750
of being tolerant of intolerance,

00:22:48.750 --> 00:22:52.410
and then therefore the intolerant
people can rise to power

00:22:52.410 --> 00:22:53.780
because you've tolerated them,

00:22:53.780 --> 00:22:54.943
and then they shut the whole system down.

00:22:54.943 --> 00:22:58.313
This is kinda the argument in simplicity.

00:22:59.770 --> 00:23:01.430
It's an issue that we should deal with,

00:23:01.430 --> 00:23:02.980
but I'm not particularly
worried about that.

00:23:02.980 --> 00:23:04.910
I think that's taking the
precautionary principle

00:23:04.910 --> 00:23:08.470
like just in case the
intolerant people come to power,

00:23:08.470 --> 00:23:10.610
we can't have that, so we should
start shutting people down.

00:23:10.610 --> 00:23:12.160
Well, who do you shut down?

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:14.020
You know, the ones that
we think are intolerant.

00:23:14.020 --> 00:23:14.853
Who, you?

00:23:14.853 --> 00:23:15.686
That you think?

00:23:15.686 --> 00:23:17.030
What, are we gonna have a committee set up

00:23:17.030 --> 00:23:18.470
that decides this?

00:23:18.470 --> 00:23:21.210
Again, pretty soon you're
shutting down this person

00:23:21.210 --> 00:23:23.040
and that person, and half
the country is shut down,

00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:24.633
and this is no good, right?

00:23:25.750 --> 00:23:27.020
I think it's a bit of a--
- But is there something then

00:23:27.020 --> 00:23:28.260
that liberalism can do?

00:23:28.260 --> 00:23:30.400
Is there something that liberalism can do

00:23:30.400 --> 00:23:33.890
to defend itself from that
because of that paradox?

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:36.840
I'll grant you that,
liberals are more open,

00:23:36.840 --> 00:23:38.820
that's the idea of liberalism

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:40.260
that comes with a lotta good stuff,

00:23:40.260 --> 00:23:43.750
like individual rights and
freedom, but then that openness,

00:23:43.750 --> 00:23:45.680
and this is exactly what
Popper was talking about,

00:23:45.680 --> 00:23:47.130
allows these guys to come in,

00:23:47.130 --> 00:23:51.110
and then liberals seem to
scatter when the going gets rough

00:23:51.110 --> 00:23:53.390
because they don't wanna be called bigots

00:23:53.390 --> 00:23:54.840
and Nazis and the rest of it.

00:23:56.410 --> 00:23:57.750
- Well, of course, no one
wants to be called that,

00:23:57.750 --> 00:24:00.570
but the problem is, is that
you have to allow people

00:24:00.570 --> 00:24:03.640
to say things like that, again,
for your own safety's sake.

00:24:03.640 --> 00:24:07.590
It's a foundational principle
of all the other rights.

00:24:07.590 --> 00:24:09.150
It stands below them.

00:24:09.150 --> 00:24:13.310
It's the foundational rock,
say the pyramid platform,

00:24:13.310 --> 00:24:15.200
upon which all the other stones are built,

00:24:15.200 --> 00:24:17.290
those being the other rights.

00:24:17.290 --> 00:24:18.530
If you don't have the freedom

00:24:18.530 --> 00:24:20.420
to think and say what you want,

00:24:20.420 --> 00:24:23.340
then all the other rights
that come from protesting,

00:24:23.340 --> 00:24:26.083
from writing, speaking,

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:29.870
better ideas to replace the bad ideas,

00:24:29.870 --> 00:24:31.690
which is pretty much the
history of civilization,

00:24:31.690 --> 00:24:33.620
is just the improvement
of our understanding

00:24:33.620 --> 00:24:35.950
of the cause of things in the world,

00:24:35.950 --> 00:24:36.920
that's the only way to do it.

00:24:36.920 --> 00:24:41.110
So to me the liberals have
to just remind themselves

00:24:41.110 --> 00:24:42.663
of that foundation principal,

00:24:44.130 --> 00:24:46.900
but also just sort of own that,

00:24:46.900 --> 00:24:49.983
as like this is where we
draw the line in the sand.

00:24:52.840 --> 00:24:55.440
This idea of diversity, so
it's a small step to say

00:24:55.440 --> 00:24:57.510
you're in favor of
diversity, oh, yeah, totally.

00:24:57.510 --> 00:24:59.780
How 'bout diversity of viewpoints?

00:24:59.780 --> 00:25:02.000
It's called viewpoint diversity.

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:03.920
You have to put that in the same bin,

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:06.203
and that's where, of course, as you know,

00:25:07.485 --> 00:25:09.590
they get a little crazy about that.

00:25:09.590 --> 00:25:10.700
- So this is a good segue for us,

00:25:10.700 --> 00:25:12.470
because what I'm finding right now

00:25:12.470 --> 00:25:15.230
is that line in the sand
that we both want drawn,

00:25:15.230 --> 00:25:18.890
it seems impossible for liberals
to do at a systemic level,

00:25:18.890 --> 00:25:22.170
meaning the individuals, we've
talked about this many times,

00:25:22.170 --> 00:25:24.300
that I believe individuals can do it,

00:25:24.300 --> 00:25:26.600
but it seems like systems based on that

00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:30.300
seem unable to stand up to it.

00:25:30.300 --> 00:25:31.580
So I thought this would be a great segue

00:25:31.580 --> 00:25:33.470
to talk about the differences

00:25:33.470 --> 00:25:34.660
that you have with Jordan Peterson,

00:25:34.660 --> 00:25:37.190
and I thought I have to
read actually the blurb

00:25:37.190 --> 00:25:38.710
that Jordan wrote on the back of the book,

00:25:38.710 --> 00:25:42.880
because I've literally never
seen a statement like this

00:25:42.880 --> 00:25:44.880
written on the back of
someone's book before,

00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:47.110
and that'll be a good launch point for us.

00:25:47.110 --> 00:25:49.760
So Jordan wrote, this is
a rather difficult book

00:25:49.760 --> 00:25:52.550
for me to blurb given
that an entire chapter

00:25:52.550 --> 00:25:55.420
is devoted to criticizing my claims

00:25:55.420 --> 00:25:59.300
about pragmatic truth
vis-a-vis scientific truth.

00:25:59.300 --> 00:26:02.490
However, Dr. Michael Shermer
is a very clear thinker,

00:26:02.490 --> 00:26:05.590
and the kind of skeptic
that is always necessary

00:26:05.590 --> 00:26:10.020
to ensure that public thought,
scientific and otherwise,

00:26:10.020 --> 00:26:11.880
maintains a certain clarity.

00:26:11.880 --> 00:26:14.260
He's a passionate advocate of free speech,

00:26:14.260 --> 00:26:16.190
for this and many other reasons,

00:26:16.190 --> 00:26:18.527
to the point of entitling his new book,

00:26:18.527 --> 00:26:20.300
"Giving the Devil His Due,"

00:26:20.300 --> 00:26:23.340
which is devoted to
many worthwhile topics,

00:26:23.340 --> 00:26:25.130
but to free speech above all.

00:26:25.130 --> 00:26:26.800
Despite our disagreements,

00:26:26.800 --> 00:26:29.370
this is a necessary book for our times.

00:26:29.370 --> 00:26:32.100
Read it, and thank God
and the powers that be

00:26:32.100 --> 00:26:33.790
that you have the right to do so.

00:26:33.790 --> 00:26:36.380
So I think it's perfect
because it's a summation

00:26:36.380 --> 00:26:38.143
of what we all know about Jordan,

00:26:39.040 --> 00:26:41.050
but the fact that you asked him to do it,

00:26:41.050 --> 00:26:42.950
despite having a chapter in here

00:26:42.950 --> 00:26:45.730
which grapples with all of his ideas,

00:26:45.730 --> 00:26:47.460
and I do think this is sort of linked

00:26:47.460 --> 00:26:49.640
to the question that I'm
asking you about liberalism.

00:26:49.640 --> 00:26:52.680
So can you can unpack
what your similarities

00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:54.280
and differences are with Jordan?

00:26:55.340 --> 00:26:58.830
- Yeah, I think when I first
saw Jordan on your show

00:26:58.830 --> 00:27:00.920
and Joe's show, and
said, "Who is this guy?"

00:27:00.920 --> 00:27:02.683
Like everybody else, "Who is this guy?"

00:27:02.683 --> 00:27:03.516
(chuckles)

00:27:03.516 --> 00:27:04.710
To me, I was dying to know

00:27:04.710 --> 00:27:06.270
what it is he thought about things,

00:27:06.270 --> 00:27:08.440
although at the same time I could see

00:27:08.440 --> 00:27:10.730
mostly liberals again
trying to shut him down

00:27:10.730 --> 00:27:12.860
or call him Hitler or whatever,

00:27:12.860 --> 00:27:15.520
a bigot and a misogynist and whatnot,

00:27:15.520 --> 00:27:17.177
and then when I met him, I thought,

00:27:17.177 --> 00:27:19.430
"This guy is a super good guy."

00:27:19.430 --> 00:27:22.120
I could see why I don't agree
with him on this and that,

00:27:22.120 --> 00:27:24.960
but he's the kind of
person we should listen to.

00:27:24.960 --> 00:27:27.810
There's a reason he
rose in such popularity,

00:27:27.810 --> 00:27:30.370
because people are
resonating with his ideas.

00:27:30.370 --> 00:27:32.960
Now, even if I don't, I
can at least recognize,

00:27:32.960 --> 00:27:35.300
well, here's a voice maybe
I should pay attention to.

00:27:35.300 --> 00:27:36.950
I might learn something from him.

00:27:38.020 --> 00:27:40.300
So the two chapters at
the end of the book,

00:27:40.300 --> 00:27:42.440
the chapter on Jordan and the
chapter on Graham Hancock,

00:27:42.440 --> 00:27:44.390
who I was on Joe Rogan's podcast with

00:27:44.390 --> 00:27:46.090
and then I got to know,
and it's like, okay,

00:27:46.090 --> 00:27:47.720
this guy's an alternative archeologist,

00:27:47.720 --> 00:27:50.460
he has some ideas that most
archeologists think are crazy

00:27:50.460 --> 00:27:53.173
or way out there or not substantiated,

00:27:54.122 --> 00:27:55.920
and yet he makes some
pretty good arguments,

00:27:55.920 --> 00:27:59.120
he's a great writer,
and from there I think

00:27:59.120 --> 00:28:02.640
this is the kind of people
we gotta give a voice to

00:28:02.640 --> 00:28:05.430
and acknowledge that
maybe we don't have time

00:28:05.430 --> 00:28:07.220
to listen to every
single person out there,

00:28:07.220 --> 00:28:10.450
but pick and choose the
ones that are very popular,

00:28:10.450 --> 00:28:12.190
because they must be saying something

00:28:12.190 --> 00:28:13.350
that resonates with people.

00:28:13.350 --> 00:28:15.190
Now, Jordan's theory of truth,

00:28:15.190 --> 00:28:18.650
it's sort of a blend of Charles
Darwin and William James,

00:28:18.650 --> 00:28:21.810
sort of an evolutionary pragmatism.

00:28:21.810 --> 00:28:24.330
I don't wanna misquote his theory.

00:28:24.330 --> 00:28:25.860
You can listen to four hours of him

00:28:25.860 --> 00:28:27.490
and say I'm kinda hashing this out,

00:28:27.490 --> 00:28:29.477
it goes on for that long,

00:28:29.477 --> 00:28:32.190
and his books kinda deconstruct all that.

00:28:32.190 --> 00:28:34.840
But near as I could tell,
it would be something like,

00:28:36.110 --> 00:28:38.860
if you believe it's true and it works,

00:28:38.860 --> 00:28:40.233
pragmatically speaking,

00:28:41.334 --> 00:28:43.740
then you can use the word truth to it.

00:28:43.740 --> 00:28:46.490
And I think it comes down
to, what do you mean by that?

00:28:47.720 --> 00:28:49.910
Was Jesus resurrected from the dead?

00:28:49.910 --> 00:28:54.910
Now, most Christians
today say yes, absolutely.

00:28:55.080 --> 00:28:59.180
Someone named Jesus
existed, he was crucified

00:28:59.180 --> 00:29:00.850
and he rose from the dead,

00:29:00.850 --> 00:29:03.050
and 100 billion people have lived and died

00:29:03.050 --> 00:29:05.410
before this 7 1/2
billion people alive now.

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:07.040
Not one of them has
come back from the dead,

00:29:07.040 --> 00:29:09.230
except this one maybe.

00:29:09.230 --> 00:29:10.560
So that's the argument.

00:29:10.560 --> 00:29:12.510
Now from a skeptic's perspective,

00:29:12.510 --> 00:29:15.390
I would say, well, that
is an extraordinary claim.

00:29:15.390 --> 00:29:16.390
How extraordinary?

00:29:16.390 --> 00:29:18.610
100 billion to one extraordinary, right?

00:29:18.610 --> 00:29:21.190
Is the evidence for it, corresponding,

00:29:21.190 --> 00:29:24.720
or in proportion to that
100 billion to one odds?

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:28.430
And the answer is no,
so I write about that.

00:29:28.430 --> 00:29:31.360
But to Jordan, I think he would say

00:29:31.360 --> 00:29:33.530
whether it happened or not is irrelevant.

00:29:33.530 --> 00:29:36.480
It's mythically true or
psychologically true.

00:29:36.480 --> 00:29:40.210
That is, you have a cross
to bear, you might say,

00:29:40.210 --> 00:29:44.180
a burden to carry of the
suffering of yourself

00:29:44.180 --> 00:29:47.350
and your family and friends
or humanity or whatever,

00:29:47.350 --> 00:29:50.050
forgiveness, starting over,

00:29:50.050 --> 00:29:54.470
this kind of resurrection
of your own life to rebuild.

00:29:54.470 --> 00:29:56.060
It's like the Noachian flood story.

00:29:56.060 --> 00:29:59.900
It's a story of
destruction and redemption.

00:29:59.900 --> 00:30:02.010
It's a kind of a born-again story.

00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:04.050
So I think what Jordan is arguing

00:30:04.050 --> 00:30:07.220
is that has mythic power
that is a kinda truth

00:30:08.377 --> 00:30:11.470
about the reality--
- That's like an eternal truth

00:30:11.470 --> 00:30:14.470
underneath the empirical truth,
something like that, right?

00:30:14.470 --> 00:30:15.660
- Right, that's right.

00:30:15.660 --> 00:30:17.740
So it could be that
people like me and Jordan,

00:30:17.740 --> 00:30:19.830
or maybe someone like
Richard Dawkins and Jordan,

00:30:19.830 --> 00:30:22.902
or whoever that's kind of an empiricist,

00:30:22.902 --> 00:30:25.162
they're just talking at two
different levels, I think.

00:30:25.162 --> 00:30:25.995
(chuckles)

00:30:25.995 --> 00:30:27.450
Jordan's not always
super clear about this,

00:30:27.450 --> 00:30:29.420
so you have to kind of read what he said

00:30:29.420 --> 00:30:30.340
here or other or whatever,

00:30:30.340 --> 00:30:32.550
but I think that's what's going on.

00:30:32.550 --> 00:30:36.060
And so to the extent that he
likes to quote Jung and Freud

00:30:36.060 --> 00:30:40.163
and Nietzsche and Tolstoy and so on,

00:30:41.761 --> 00:30:43.120
and Shakespeare, Jane Austen,

00:30:43.120 --> 00:30:45.140
the reason this is great literature

00:30:45.140 --> 00:30:47.190
is because it's tapping something

00:30:47.190 --> 00:30:49.040
about the reality of human nature,

00:30:49.040 --> 00:30:51.580
human relationships, human societies,

00:30:51.580 --> 00:30:54.790
conflict, power struggles, sex,

00:30:54.790 --> 00:30:59.790
and gossip, and conflict,
and violence, war.

00:30:59.970 --> 00:31:02.490
Those are all realities
that everybody knows,

00:31:02.490 --> 00:31:05.900
and these great novelists
can tap into that.

00:31:05.900 --> 00:31:06.900
There's a whole study now

00:31:06.900 --> 00:31:11.770
of evolutionary psychology of literature,

00:31:11.770 --> 00:31:15.410
where they do a content
analysis of great works

00:31:15.410 --> 00:31:18.480
to see which themes come
up over and over and over,

00:31:18.480 --> 00:31:21.040
and the deepest themes
that we call care about,

00:31:21.040 --> 00:31:25.310
again the sort of hierarchy,
social status, power,

00:31:25.310 --> 00:31:29.023
deception, honesty, love, sex, all that.

00:31:30.853 --> 00:31:32.530
And so there I think
what Jordan is arguing

00:31:32.530 --> 00:31:35.180
that I would agree with,
that there are truths there,

00:31:36.060 --> 00:31:38.440
but that's different
than an empirical truth.

00:31:38.440 --> 00:31:41.670
So the analogy I make is like
people will often send me,

00:31:41.670 --> 00:31:42.790
as the editor of "Skeptic" magazine,

00:31:42.790 --> 00:31:46.390
articles explaining what
really happened with the flood,

00:31:46.390 --> 00:31:49.030
a meteor strike or it
was a volcano eruption,

00:31:49.030 --> 00:31:52.270
not the flood, but the
parting of the Red Sea say,

00:31:52.270 --> 00:31:55.590
but the flood also, or
Jesus was on the cross,

00:31:55.590 --> 00:31:58.200
and then somebody gave him
this chemical through the rag

00:31:58.200 --> 00:31:59.680
and that put him into a coma,

00:31:59.680 --> 00:32:02.989
and then they put him in the
tomb, and then he woke up.

00:32:02.989 --> 00:32:03.822
- Many versions of this.
- And then his disciples

00:32:03.822 --> 00:32:05.060
whisked him off to France.

00:32:06.320 --> 00:32:09.390
Yeah, and he married Mary
Magdalene and they had kids,

00:32:09.390 --> 00:32:10.720
and the bloodline is still around.

00:32:10.720 --> 00:32:12.410
This was of course Dan Brown's novel.

00:32:12.410 --> 00:32:13.540
That was a real book, actually.

00:32:13.540 --> 00:32:14.650
Before Dan Brown, there was a book

00:32:14.650 --> 00:32:16.250
called "Holy Blood, Holy Grail."

00:32:17.290 --> 00:32:20.470
Actually, those guys sued Dan
Brown for stealing their idea.

00:32:20.470 --> 00:32:21.949
He's like, "It's a novel, dude."

00:32:21.949 --> 00:32:22.782
(laughing)

00:32:22.782 --> 00:32:25.000
- Huh, is that right?
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:32:25.000 --> 00:32:26.050
He sold so many books.

00:32:26.050 --> 00:32:27.840
Man, if you don't sell any books,

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:29.820
no one's gonna sue you
'cause there's no money.

00:32:29.820 --> 00:32:31.220
So it was kinda funny.

00:32:31.220 --> 00:32:32.590
But there you saw the difference.

00:32:32.590 --> 00:32:36.290
Dan Brown's novel resonated
'cause it's just a story.

00:32:36.290 --> 00:32:38.470
He's not claiming its empirically true,

00:32:38.470 --> 00:32:41.390
but these guys, "The Holy
Blood, Holy Grail" authors,

00:32:41.390 --> 00:32:43.970
they were saying, "No,
no, this really happened."

00:32:43.970 --> 00:32:45.640
Okay, so those are two different ways

00:32:45.640 --> 00:32:47.783
of saying really happened or truth.

00:32:50.020 --> 00:32:53.400
- So do you think it's possible
that we need both of those?

00:32:53.400 --> 00:32:56.790
I think maybe that's the position
that I've come around to,

00:32:56.790 --> 00:32:58.380
that we need empirical truths,

00:32:58.380 --> 00:33:01.320
we need scientific truths absolutely,

00:33:01.320 --> 00:33:03.760
but when you talk about that other layer

00:33:03.760 --> 00:33:06.500
that Jordan's talking about,
the time-tested things

00:33:06.500 --> 00:33:10.200
about purpose and
happiness and individualism

00:33:10.200 --> 00:33:12.870
and the drive to accomplish what humans

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:15.970
are sort of here to do,
that for me I've come to,

00:33:15.970 --> 00:33:18.270
and this is one of the spots
when I was just on your show

00:33:18.270 --> 00:33:20.690
you were asking about, 'cause
I write about this in my book,

00:33:20.690 --> 00:33:22.940
I've come to the place where for society

00:33:22.940 --> 00:33:24.930
to organize itself properly,

00:33:24.930 --> 00:33:28.820
it does need some other ethos beneath it.

00:33:28.820 --> 00:33:31.080
And what I think is something

00:33:31.080 --> 00:33:32.730
like when you talk about Shakespeare

00:33:32.730 --> 00:33:35.160
and all the authors that
write about these themes too,

00:33:35.160 --> 00:33:36.610
that in and of themselves,

00:33:36.610 --> 00:33:40.540
those stories aren't enough
to organize a society around.

00:33:40.540 --> 00:33:43.310
Not that again that
individuals can't do it,

00:33:43.310 --> 00:33:46.700
but for actually for a
society to function properly.

00:33:46.700 --> 00:33:48.700
Do you think there's some truth to that?

00:33:50.140 --> 00:33:51.530
- Yeah, I do, for sure.

00:33:51.530 --> 00:33:56.530
There's a reason why drama
films and television sells

00:33:56.580 --> 00:34:01.110
or has much bigger viewerships
than documentary films.

00:34:01.110 --> 00:34:04.610
It's very rare any documentary
film gets millions of views.

00:34:04.610 --> 00:34:06.220
There's a few, but not many.

00:34:06.220 --> 00:34:09.430
And the reason is because
we're storytelling animals

00:34:09.430 --> 00:34:13.020
and we're moved emotionally,
deeply psychologically moved

00:34:13.020 --> 00:34:16.110
by a story about some person.

00:34:16.110 --> 00:34:19.427
Like when Spielberg
made "Schindler's List,"

00:34:20.510 --> 00:34:22.100
it's not the story of the Holocaust

00:34:22.100 --> 00:34:24.383
because that's too big a subject.

00:34:25.492 --> 00:34:27.790
You could have like a
Ken Burn's 13-part series

00:34:27.790 --> 00:34:29.750
on the Holocaust, people have done this,

00:34:29.750 --> 00:34:30.760
'cause there's so many stories to tell.

00:34:30.760 --> 00:34:32.580
Instead, he just took one little story.

00:34:32.580 --> 00:34:35.570
This guy, Oskar Schindler,
who was this guy?

00:34:35.570 --> 00:34:37.310
Keneally wrote that novel about him,

00:34:37.310 --> 00:34:41.120
sort of a semi-true novel.

00:34:41.120 --> 00:34:43.030
If you really look at it,
it's not that interesting,

00:34:43.030 --> 00:34:45.160
the story, but when you
bring it on the big screen

00:34:45.160 --> 00:34:47.570
and then you build in the
Holocaust around that story,

00:34:47.570 --> 00:34:49.840
but it's the narrative
thread that goes throughout.

00:34:49.840 --> 00:34:51.570
So all filmmakers know this.

00:34:51.570 --> 00:34:53.070
Most of us nonfiction writers,

00:34:53.070 --> 00:34:55.100
we don't understand you
gotta have a narrative story,

00:34:55.100 --> 00:34:59.210
you gotta have an arc to it
that pulls readers along.

00:34:59.210 --> 00:35:03.420
Good writers do that and
good filmmakers do this.

00:35:03.420 --> 00:35:06.750
So there is something in the human mind

00:35:06.750 --> 00:35:11.750
about grasping ideas and
holding them in a way,

00:35:12.770 --> 00:35:15.640
yammering away at a
blackboard giving a lecture,

00:35:15.640 --> 00:35:18.820
which I do for a living,
that's not nearly as powerful

00:35:18.820 --> 00:35:21.220
as somebody who could tell just one story

00:35:21.220 --> 00:35:24.400
about this one person who
had this happen to him,

00:35:24.400 --> 00:35:27.690
and then you embed in there
some ethical principle

00:35:27.690 --> 00:35:28.610
or some conflict.

00:35:28.610 --> 00:35:30.000
Essentially, you could tell the story

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:33.440
of the trolley problem in some drama

00:35:33.440 --> 00:35:35.150
and that would be far more effective

00:35:35.150 --> 00:35:37.760
than me giving an explanation
with a PowerPoint slide

00:35:37.760 --> 00:35:39.450
showing the guy on the track

00:35:39.450 --> 00:35:41.259
and the five workers and the one worker,

00:35:41.259 --> 00:35:43.080
(laughing)

00:35:43.080 --> 00:35:45.883
or yammering on about Kant categorically.

00:35:47.840 --> 00:35:51.070
- So basically, if you
could spend five years

00:35:51.070 --> 00:35:53.070
studying at Cambridge with the greatest

00:35:53.070 --> 00:35:54.910
biblical scholars of all time,

00:35:54.910 --> 00:35:57.100
and you could get them to sort of...

00:35:57.100 --> 00:35:59.800
If you could find a
scholar who would basically

00:35:59.800 --> 00:36:04.010
remove all of the stuff that
can't be empirically true,

00:36:04.010 --> 00:36:07.950
empirically proved, sorry, but
that the lessons were lessons

00:36:07.950 --> 00:36:11.580
that you believed to be mystically true,

00:36:11.580 --> 00:36:14.549
you could come around to
those stories, basically.

00:36:14.549 --> 00:36:16.100
You don't think those things
are mutually exclusive.

00:36:16.100 --> 00:36:19.120
That's I think really
what I'm trying to get.

00:36:19.120 --> 00:36:20.483
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:36:21.454 --> 00:36:23.970
I just had Bart Ehrman on my podcast

00:36:23.970 --> 00:36:25.850
and asked him this very thing,

00:36:25.850 --> 00:36:27.972
'cause he was a Christian for a long time,

00:36:27.972 --> 00:36:28.805
and now he's an atheist.

00:36:28.805 --> 00:36:29.657
But I said, "Could you be a Christian

00:36:29.657 --> 00:36:33.007
"and not believe in the
literal resurrection of Jesus?

00:36:33.007 --> 00:36:34.367
"I think it's mythically true,

00:36:34.367 --> 00:36:35.347
"psychologically true, whatever,"

00:36:35.347 --> 00:36:37.427
and he goes, "Yeah, lots of Christians

00:36:37.427 --> 00:36:38.490
"used to believe that."

00:36:38.490 --> 00:36:41.320
That was the main thing until really

00:36:41.320 --> 00:36:43.120
just the last century and a half or so

00:36:43.120 --> 00:36:46.670
with the rise of science
and the power of science.

00:36:46.670 --> 00:36:49.067
Believers feel like,
"Well, I can't just say

00:36:49.067 --> 00:36:52.567
"it's true for me or
it's my faith tradition

00:36:52.567 --> 00:36:53.767
"and I can't prove it."

00:36:54.830 --> 00:36:55.940
There's a sense that now

00:36:55.940 --> 00:36:57.640
you actually have to make an argument,

00:36:57.640 --> 00:36:59.510
like this is why it's really true,

00:36:59.510 --> 00:37:01.540
not just psychologically
true and true for me,

00:37:01.540 --> 00:37:03.010
my faith tradition.

00:37:03.010 --> 00:37:04.940
So creationists went down this road

00:37:04.940 --> 00:37:07.430
in the early 20th century.

00:37:07.430 --> 00:37:09.810
We have to argue that the
flood really happened.

00:37:09.810 --> 00:37:11.720
Okay, that means we gotta study geology,

00:37:11.720 --> 00:37:14.060
we gotta figure out how
floods do this to that

00:37:14.060 --> 00:37:17.070
and rocks and blah, and
they're still doing that

00:37:17.070 --> 00:37:19.280
to a certain extent, and
it never gets them anywhere

00:37:19.280 --> 00:37:21.750
because, A, they don't
have very good arguments,

00:37:21.750 --> 00:37:26.010
B, science has better arguments
for the flood or whatever,

00:37:26.010 --> 00:37:28.500
and in any case, once
you go down that road

00:37:28.500 --> 00:37:31.970
and you go, well, what happens
if science fills that gap,

00:37:31.970 --> 00:37:34.360
are you gonna abandon
your Christian faith?

00:37:34.360 --> 00:37:36.570
Well, no, because that's
not why I believe.

00:37:36.570 --> 00:37:39.320
Right.
(chuckling)

00:37:39.320 --> 00:37:41.070
- Have you found some
people like that though,

00:37:41.070 --> 00:37:45.950
that they get to that place
and science fills the hole,

00:37:45.950 --> 00:37:47.610
and then they suddenly walk?

00:37:47.610 --> 00:37:50.160
Or do you find that more
people walk from religion

00:37:50.160 --> 00:37:51.910
because of some other inconsistency

00:37:51.910 --> 00:37:54.810
or some other personal ax to
grind or something like that?

00:37:56.150 --> 00:37:59.033
- For personal reasons is
the main reason people leave.

00:38:00.430 --> 00:38:03.010
There's this meme saying you can't reason

00:38:03.010 --> 00:38:04.560
somebody out of something
they didn't reason

00:38:04.560 --> 00:38:06.160
their way into in the first place.

00:38:06.160 --> 00:38:06.993
That's not true.

00:38:08.600 --> 00:38:09.520
We do this all the time,

00:38:09.520 --> 00:38:11.370
skeptics and scientists or whatever,

00:38:11.370 --> 00:38:13.760
and the research from Hugo Mercier

00:38:13.760 --> 00:38:16.020
and other cognitive
psychologists that study this

00:38:16.020 --> 00:38:18.160
show that you can talk
people out of beliefs,

00:38:18.160 --> 00:38:19.540
you can reason them out.

00:38:19.540 --> 00:38:21.080
But just collectively on average,

00:38:21.080 --> 00:38:22.740
I think most people come to religion

00:38:22.740 --> 00:38:24.920
or they leave religion
for personal reasons.

00:38:24.920 --> 00:38:26.830
They come there, they
were raised that way,

00:38:26.830 --> 00:38:28.180
or their spouse is that way,

00:38:28.180 --> 00:38:32.180
or their peer group in
high school, like my case,

00:38:32.180 --> 00:38:34.550
and they leave for other personal reasons.

00:38:34.550 --> 00:38:37.030
Like after the Holocaust, a
lotta Jews became atheists,

00:38:37.030 --> 00:38:40.803
like where was God, the
problem of theodicy again.

00:38:42.452 --> 00:38:44.502
And in my case, when I finally gave it up

00:38:45.610 --> 00:38:48.720
after seven years at the
end of graduate school,

00:38:48.720 --> 00:38:51.590
I didn't tell anybody, I
just quit talking about it.

00:38:51.590 --> 00:38:53.550
And I think my family was kinda relieved,

00:38:53.550 --> 00:38:56.693
because I was always
yammering away about Jesus.

00:38:57.650 --> 00:38:59.860
Witnessing is what it's
called, witnessing.

00:38:59.860 --> 00:39:01.577
And I think they were sick
of it, so they were like,

00:39:01.577 --> 00:39:03.933
"Thank God, Michael stopped
talking about that."

00:39:03.933 --> 00:39:05.480
(laughing)

00:39:05.480 --> 00:39:07.940
And I think that's usually what happens.

00:39:09.130 --> 00:39:10.960
- So as a guy that now talks
about some other stuff,

00:39:10.960 --> 00:39:12.500
the subtitle of the book

00:39:12.500 --> 00:39:15.230
is "Reflections of a Scientific Humanist,"

00:39:15.230 --> 00:39:17.800
and you mentioned that phrase earlier.

00:39:17.800 --> 00:39:19.650
Can you explain exactly what that is?

00:39:21.260 --> 00:39:25.910
- Yeah, so humanism has been
around really for a long time.

00:39:25.910 --> 00:39:27.650
Secular humanism is a movement,

00:39:27.650 --> 00:39:29.640
it kinda rose in the '30s and '40s,

00:39:29.640 --> 00:39:34.640
very left-wing liberal, again,
free speech, free thought,

00:39:36.170 --> 00:39:41.010
women's rights, civil rights,
African American rights,

00:39:41.010 --> 00:39:44.250
and on and on, and so that was great.

00:39:44.250 --> 00:39:47.713
But then by the '90s and early 2000s,

00:39:49.132 --> 00:39:51.690
it shifted not just further to the left,

00:39:51.690 --> 00:39:55.420
but also this kinda tribal
ticking of the boxes,

00:39:55.420 --> 00:39:59.250
like here are the 12 things
secular humanists believe.

00:39:59.250 --> 00:40:02.610
Well, I can tick like
eight of the 12 boxes.

00:40:02.610 --> 00:40:03.810
Can I still be a humanist?

00:40:03.810 --> 00:40:06.430
And it became, no, you're out.

00:40:06.430 --> 00:40:09.236
If you don't tick all
12 boxes, you're out!

00:40:09.236 --> 00:40:10.069
(laughing)

00:40:10.069 --> 00:40:12.798
I forget what you called
it, but it was the narcism

00:40:12.798 --> 00:40:14.900
of small differences?
- Well, it's the narcism

00:40:14.900 --> 00:40:16.783
of small differences, yeah.

00:40:17.633 --> 00:40:18.466
- Yeah, yeah, I love that phrase.

00:40:18.466 --> 00:40:20.020
- The narcism of small differences.

00:40:20.020 --> 00:40:21.020
It's a great phrase.

00:40:21.887 --> 00:40:23.080
- That's not mine originally.

00:40:23.080 --> 00:40:24.487
I don't know who came up with it.

00:40:24.487 --> 00:40:25.423
- Oh, okay, all right.

00:40:28.040 --> 00:40:30.680
And then Pinker started calling
it enlightenment humanism.

00:40:30.680 --> 00:40:33.450
I like that, sometimes I use that phrase,

00:40:33.450 --> 00:40:34.900
but really in those chapters,

00:40:34.900 --> 00:40:37.720
I'm arguing that science
has something to say

00:40:37.720 --> 00:40:39.790
about human values and morals

00:40:39.790 --> 00:40:41.840
and ethical theories and so on,

00:40:41.840 --> 00:40:46.030
even if you can't perfectly
debunk the naturalistic fallacy

00:40:46.030 --> 00:40:48.600
and go from is to ought,

00:40:48.600 --> 00:40:51.390
the way things are to the
way things they ought to be.

00:40:51.390 --> 00:40:53.100
Maybe technically that's not possible,

00:40:53.100 --> 00:40:54.883
but again, I don't care about that.

00:40:55.890 --> 00:40:58.190
I'm just saying this binary choice

00:40:58.190 --> 00:41:00.350
between divine command theory,

00:41:00.350 --> 00:41:03.930
that is God said it and that
makes it right or wrong,

00:41:03.930 --> 00:41:06.090
versus relativism, anything goes,

00:41:06.090 --> 00:41:08.580
there are no real moral values,

00:41:08.580 --> 00:41:10.373
I just don't believe that.

00:41:11.580 --> 00:41:13.580
I quote Abraham Lincoln,
if slavery is not wrong,

00:41:13.580 --> 00:41:14.860
nothing is wrong.

00:41:14.860 --> 00:41:16.720
And I can say if the
Holocaust is not wrong,

00:41:16.720 --> 00:41:17.553
nothing is wrong.

00:41:17.553 --> 00:41:19.027
Show me a society where people say,

00:41:19.027 --> 00:41:20.857
"I really wanna be enslaved,"

00:41:22.627 --> 00:41:24.367
or most people in a group say,

00:41:24.367 --> 00:41:26.570
"Yeah, I really think we
should be exterminated."

00:41:26.570 --> 00:41:28.470
This is insane, nobody thinks that.

00:41:28.470 --> 00:41:29.990
We all know why that's wrong.

00:41:29.990 --> 00:41:31.940
We're born knowing that that's wrong.

00:41:31.940 --> 00:41:35.250
Now, set aside crazy psychopaths

00:41:35.250 --> 00:41:36.690
that just like murdering people,

00:41:36.690 --> 00:41:38.480
most of us have a sense
of right and wrong.

00:41:38.480 --> 00:41:39.960
That comes from our nature.

00:41:39.960 --> 00:41:41.880
So I have long arguments
about the evolutionary

00:41:41.880 --> 00:41:43.887
origins of morality and so forth,

00:41:43.887 --> 00:41:47.390
but the idea again is just to
counter this binary choice.

00:41:47.390 --> 00:41:48.570
There's more of that.

00:41:48.570 --> 00:41:50.180
So I call it scientific humanism

00:41:50.180 --> 00:41:51.470
or call it enlightenment humanism.

00:41:51.470 --> 00:41:55.650
But let's not make the tent so small

00:41:55.650 --> 00:41:58.063
that only a few people
can tick all 12 boxes.

00:41:58.982 --> 00:42:01.170
That's a bad road to go
down, and for some reason,

00:42:01.170 --> 00:42:03.360
a lotta social movements do that.

00:42:03.360 --> 00:42:05.410
I remember when
objectivism was on the rise

00:42:05.410 --> 00:42:06.690
when I was at Pepperdine.

00:42:06.690 --> 00:42:08.890
I mentioned everybody
read "Atlas Shrugged,"

00:42:08.890 --> 00:42:09.723
right?
- Yeah.

00:42:10.561 --> 00:42:13.979
- And then she died in '82, so
there was this big movement,

00:42:13.979 --> 00:42:16.730
but then the biographies
about her came out

00:42:16.730 --> 00:42:18.750
that showed the movement was shrinking

00:42:18.750 --> 00:42:20.920
as they were kicking people out.

00:42:20.920 --> 00:42:23.930
You watch the wrong films,
you listen to the wrong music.

00:42:23.930 --> 00:42:25.710
That's not objectivist music.

00:42:25.710 --> 00:42:27.220
(laughing) What?

00:42:27.220 --> 00:42:29.140
Even smoking the wrong brand of cigarettes

00:42:29.140 --> 00:42:33.100
became the non-objectivist
form of cigarette smoking.

00:42:33.100 --> 00:42:35.190
Pretty soon, there's like
six people at her funeral.

00:42:35.190 --> 00:42:36.190
There's nobody left.

00:42:37.373 --> 00:42:38.650
- You know, it's so
interesting, 'cause as you know,

00:42:38.650 --> 00:42:41.690
I do some speaking events
with the Ayn Rand Institute.

00:42:41.690 --> 00:42:43.460
I think we've done at least
one together, didn't we?

00:42:43.460 --> 00:42:45.550
I think we did something
with Ayn Rand together,

00:42:45.550 --> 00:42:47.070
maybe at USC or something,

00:42:47.070 --> 00:42:50.200
and I think actually the top brass there,

00:42:50.200 --> 00:42:51.033
I don't wanna speak for them,

00:42:51.033 --> 00:42:52.630
but I think they really do regret

00:42:52.630 --> 00:42:54.340
some of exactly what you're talking about,

00:42:54.340 --> 00:42:56.810
making it so hysterical in nature.

00:42:56.810 --> 00:42:58.520
But you know when you
mentioned Ayn Rand before

00:42:58.520 --> 00:43:00.380
and being at Pepperdine,
it is sort of interesting,

00:43:00.380 --> 00:43:02.300
because you have all these people

00:43:02.300 --> 00:43:06.080
that come from a religious
background reading a book

00:43:06.080 --> 00:43:09.380
about really from the ultimate
individualist capitalist

00:43:09.380 --> 00:43:11.180
who truly was a secularist too,

00:43:11.180 --> 00:43:13.970
because she did a mass
dissection of religion

00:43:13.970 --> 00:43:16.210
and really was completely anti-religion,

00:43:16.210 --> 00:43:18.720
yet somehow these people were
able to read these things,

00:43:18.720 --> 00:43:21.550
and I guess it didn't work
out that well in the end,

00:43:21.550 --> 00:43:24.070
but they were trying to
put these pieces together,

00:43:24.070 --> 00:43:25.420
which is kinda interesting.

00:43:26.610 --> 00:43:30.750
- Yeah, for sure, the new
generation of Ayn Rand supporters

00:43:30.750 --> 00:43:32.563
are much more ecumenical about that.

00:43:33.470 --> 00:43:36.460
I think in the earlier
generation when Rand was alive,

00:43:36.460 --> 00:43:40.804
I think they said Friedrich
Hayek and Ludwig von Mises

00:43:40.804 --> 00:43:43.750
and Milton Friedman were
basically socialist.

00:43:43.750 --> 00:43:45.222
It's like, what?

00:43:45.222 --> 00:43:46.055
(laughing)

00:43:46.055 --> 00:43:47.160
These guys are like hardcore libertarian.

00:43:47.160 --> 00:43:49.329
They're not libertarian
enough, you're out!

00:43:49.329 --> 00:43:51.610
(laughing)

00:43:51.610 --> 00:43:53.830
- No, seriously.
- Although, according to...

00:43:54.790 --> 00:43:56.149
Well, according to "The New York Times,"

00:43:56.149 --> 00:43:58.740
Milton Friedman's leading
people to the alt-right,

00:43:58.740 --> 00:43:59.573
didn't you know that?

00:43:59.573 --> 00:44:02.000
I mean, that's how it works.
- That's right, that's right.

00:44:02.000 --> 00:44:04.633
But again, I think all
social movements do that.

00:44:08.410 --> 00:44:09.340
- We only have a little bit of time left,

00:44:09.340 --> 00:44:12.100
I wanna move on to some of
the ways that you tackle

00:44:12.100 --> 00:44:13.920
the issues of the day through this lens.

00:44:13.920 --> 00:44:15.450
So let's talk about guns a little bit,

00:44:15.450 --> 00:44:18.770
because basically you talk
about it's sorta just the lens

00:44:18.770 --> 00:44:21.070
that liberals and conservatives are using

00:44:21.070 --> 00:44:23.430
to look at the issue
that is sorta the problem

00:44:23.430 --> 00:44:24.423
in the first place.

00:44:25.920 --> 00:44:27.710
- Yeah, guns, I have a couple
of chapters in the book

00:44:27.710 --> 00:44:30.150
on guns because I did these
debates with John Lott,

00:44:30.150 --> 00:44:32.510
the author of that book,
"More Guns, Less Crime."

00:44:32.510 --> 00:44:33.870
So he's pretty hardcore.

00:44:33.870 --> 00:44:34.910
You see him a lot on TV

00:44:34.910 --> 00:44:37.470
defending the Second Amendment and so on.

00:44:37.470 --> 00:44:40.710
Okay, fine, I'm not a critic
of the Second Amendment.

00:44:40.710 --> 00:44:44.210
I'm concerned about the
levels of gun violence.

00:44:44.210 --> 00:44:47.100
It's 35 to 40,000 a year die of guns.

00:44:47.100 --> 00:44:48.720
Shouldn't we do something about this?

00:44:48.720 --> 00:44:50.610
The automobile industry did this.

00:44:50.610 --> 00:44:53.193
Let's put seatbelts in
and airbags and so on.

00:44:54.248 --> 00:44:55.770
And why doesn't the gun
manufacturers do this?

00:44:55.770 --> 00:44:57.050
The NRA used to do that.

00:44:57.050 --> 00:44:59.490
The NRA was a gun safety club

00:45:00.460 --> 00:45:03.160
that insisted on training
people on the use of guns.

00:45:03.160 --> 00:45:05.790
Okay, I'm fine with guns as
long as you have training

00:45:05.790 --> 00:45:07.910
and you keep it in a locked box and so on.

00:45:07.910 --> 00:45:11.180
But the problem is, is
that I think the right now

00:45:11.180 --> 00:45:13.570
has fetishized the Second Amendment

00:45:13.570 --> 00:45:16.210
such that guns are a
proxy for something else.

00:45:16.210 --> 00:45:18.140
It's not the guns they're defending.

00:45:18.140 --> 00:45:22.030
It's that guns represent
freedom, autonomy, liberty,

00:45:22.030 --> 00:45:24.930
the right for me to protect myself

00:45:24.930 --> 00:45:26.370
and my family and my home.

00:45:26.370 --> 00:45:28.520
Well, isn't that what
law enforcement is for?

00:45:28.520 --> 00:45:30.060
Yeah, but they can't be there on time,

00:45:30.060 --> 00:45:32.000
and they're not always reliable.

00:45:32.000 --> 00:45:32.877
Therefore, it's up to me.

00:45:32.877 --> 00:45:36.740
That's a very American way
of thinking about this issue.

00:45:36.740 --> 00:45:41.430
And so I look at some of
those issues in the book, too.

00:45:41.430 --> 00:45:42.470
It's a hard one, I know,

00:45:42.470 --> 00:45:45.460
because I have no solution to this, Dave.

00:45:45.460 --> 00:45:47.410
There's 350 million guns in America.

00:45:47.410 --> 00:45:49.120
We're not gonna confiscate them all.

00:45:49.120 --> 00:45:51.390
The Second Amendment's
not gonna be tossed out.

00:45:51.390 --> 00:45:52.590
It's never gonna happen.

00:45:54.390 --> 00:45:57.580
But the idea, like shouldn't
we just have background checks

00:45:57.580 --> 00:46:00.480
and close the gun show loophole,
and just little things?

00:46:00.480 --> 00:46:02.430
But the moment you go down that path,

00:46:02.430 --> 00:46:05.180
the people on far end of that spectrum,

00:46:05.180 --> 00:46:06.487
they go, "Oh, no, that's a slippery slope

00:46:06.487 --> 00:46:08.857
"and pretty soon it's Hitlers
marching in the streets

00:46:08.857 --> 00:46:10.407
"taking away everybody's guns."

00:46:11.790 --> 00:46:15.160
Come on, we should at least
be able to talk about it.

00:46:15.160 --> 00:46:16.100
- Yeah, there's so much there.

00:46:16.100 --> 00:46:17.790
I mean, 350 million guns as you say,

00:46:17.790 --> 00:46:19.700
that's more than people
in the United States,

00:46:19.700 --> 00:46:20.690
that's one thing,

00:46:20.690 --> 00:46:23.250
but do you think in a time
like we're in right now,

00:46:23.250 --> 00:46:25.070
where the reason we're
not doing this in studio

00:46:25.070 --> 00:46:26.740
is obviously because of coronavirus,

00:46:26.740 --> 00:46:28.810
and it's pretty miraculous
we've talked for 50 minutes

00:46:28.810 --> 00:46:31.290
and not even mentioned it, which is nice,

00:46:31.290 --> 00:46:34.300
but that in a time like right
now where we sees stories

00:46:34.300 --> 00:46:36.360
where New York City's letting out

00:46:36.360 --> 00:46:38.030
a certain amount of prisoners,

00:46:38.030 --> 00:46:40.440
and we're closing gun shops in LA,

00:46:40.440 --> 00:46:42.870
and the government seems
to be taking these,

00:46:42.870 --> 00:46:45.720
what I would say are strange,
somewhat radical measures,

00:46:45.720 --> 00:46:49.550
that it actually feeds
that very American idea,

00:46:49.550 --> 00:46:51.140
even if you're not
thrilled with that idea,

00:46:51.140 --> 00:46:53.670
that you could see why someone

00:46:53.670 --> 00:46:55.273
would think the way they think?

00:46:56.350 --> 00:46:58.103
- Yeah, yeah, for sure.

00:46:59.230 --> 00:47:01.510
A couple of gun rights groups sued Newsom

00:47:01.510 --> 00:47:02.900
and the state of California,

00:47:02.900 --> 00:47:07.000
insisting that gun shops
are a essential commodity,

00:47:07.000 --> 00:47:10.250
like pharmacies and
supermarkets should stay open,

00:47:10.250 --> 00:47:11.690
so should gun shops.

00:47:11.690 --> 00:47:15.400
Wow, okay, really? (chuckles)

00:47:15.400 --> 00:47:18.340
Do you really believe,
even if the great America

00:47:18.340 --> 00:47:20.960
is on the precipice of
collapsing at any moment,

00:47:20.960 --> 00:47:22.440
the currency's gonna be useless

00:47:22.440 --> 00:47:24.030
and within a matter of weeks,

00:47:24.030 --> 00:47:25.773
there'll be mobs in the street?

00:47:25.773 --> 00:47:29.300
I better packing heat and have my home

00:47:29.300 --> 00:47:31.840
fully loaded up and ready to shoot people.

00:47:31.840 --> 00:47:33.870
Come on, don't you have more confidence

00:47:33.870 --> 00:47:35.960
in the American system of law and order

00:47:35.960 --> 00:47:38.000
and civil society than that?

00:47:38.000 --> 00:47:39.993
And apparently, some people don't.

00:47:41.533 --> 00:47:45.020
- I think it's shaking, I really do.

00:47:45.020 --> 00:47:48.600
Listen, I wish we could go longer,

00:47:48.600 --> 00:47:50.060
but we're crunched over here.

00:47:50.060 --> 00:47:52.270
But we will do this in
person the second we can,

00:47:52.270 --> 00:47:54.400
and we'll pick it up, 'cause
there's so much more in here.

00:47:54.400 --> 00:47:57.150
It's a dense, thoughtful
book, and I really enjoyed it.

00:47:58.110 --> 00:47:59.920
This is now at least
your seventh appearance

00:47:59.920 --> 00:48:01.170
on "The Rubin Report,"

00:48:01.170 --> 00:48:03.453
so I suspect it will continue.
- Well, thanks, Dave.

00:48:04.990 --> 00:48:07.200
No, you're one of the people I think of

00:48:07.200 --> 00:48:08.033
with "Giving the Devil."

00:48:08.033 --> 00:48:10.830
You do give the Devil their due
and you get hammered for it.

00:48:10.830 --> 00:48:14.280
But you gotta absorb it,
because that's the only way.

00:48:14.280 --> 00:48:15.270
I listen to your show all the time.

00:48:15.270 --> 00:48:16.490
There's people I never even heard of.

00:48:16.490 --> 00:48:19.670
It's like, wow, that's an
incredibly interesting idea.

00:48:19.670 --> 00:48:23.053
That's the only way we can
learn and make progress.

00:48:24.770 --> 00:48:25.603
- Indeed, my friend.

00:48:25.603 --> 00:48:28.190
Well, I look forward to
seeing you in person,

00:48:28.190 --> 00:48:30.570
and it'll be better done
than through these pipes

00:48:30.570 --> 00:48:31.550
that Al Gore developed.

00:48:31.550 --> 00:48:33.610
So, good seeing you,
Michael, thanks so much.

